# The Pues Manifesto

Ten principles that apply to every Pues project — design, code, docs, and
tooling. They are the shared "why" behind the per-task skills; when a decision
is unclear, these decide it. They are ordered by precedence: when two collide,
the higher one wins. (Several are already used as shorthand in code/docs, e.g.
`STUF`, `POLS`.)

1. **BAIT — Build AI Tools.** Build for agents as first-class users: `--json`,
   stable exit codes, machine-readable contracts, headless paths alongside the
   UI. The test: an agent can drive the feature end-to-end from its docs and
   contracts alone — if it needs a human demonstration, it fails BAIT.
2. **CEDE — Constrain Every Delegated Executor.** The authenticated human is the
   trust root with full authority. Delegates acting for them get only the
   capabilities they need: security by design.
3. **POLS — Principle Of Least Surprise.** Match what people already know (Git
   verbs, platform idioms, existing patterns). No silent magic; behaviour
   should read the way its name promises. Trust is the foundation.
4. **STUF — Show Them Useful Feedback.** Every failure names what happened and
   a literally-runnable way to recover. Errors are actionable, never dead ends.
5. **KISS — Keep It Simple, Stupid.** Prefer the smallest design that works;
   lean on Bun internals and convention over configuration. The simplest thing
   that satisfies the above — not simpler.
6. **DOTE — Do One Thing Excellently.** Each tool, skill, command, and module
   has one clear job done well — not several jobs done adequately.
7. **FIAT — Fit It All Together.** The separate repos and parts compose into one
   coherent whole through shared contracts — a capability built once drops into
   any consumer, vendored rather than reinvented per repo.
8. **WACO — We Are Customer One.** Dogfood everything. If it's awkward for us,
   it ships awkward — fix it where we feel it first.
9. **DAPP — Document All Pain Points.** When something is rough or a tool can't
   do X, that's a finding — capture it (an issue/note), don't route around it
   silently.
10. **FLOW — Feedback Loops On Workflows.** Tighten the find→fix→ship loop until
    improvement is the default state. Kaizen as an emergent property of the
    system, never a scheduled event.
